# Copy to .env for a local `docker compose up`. On the VPS these are set in Dokploy, not in a file.

# Client site origins allowed to post feedback, comma-separated. `*` allows any origin — fine for a
# preview deploy, wrong in production.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which store holds the seeds: `linear` (the default), `sqlite`, or `memory`.
#
# An unknown name is refused at boot rather than defaulted, and `memory` is refused under
# NODE_ENV=production — feedback kept in RAM behind a green health check is worse than a worker that
# will not start. Each store reads only its own variables, so a worker on SQLite is never asked for a
# Linear key and vice versa.
# FRUITBACK_STORE=linear

# ── FRUITBACK_STORE=sqlite ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One file, no account anywhere, no dependency: `node:sqlite` is built into Node. The schema is
# created on first open and migrated in place, so there is no separate command to run. Put it on a
# mounted volume — /data is created in the image for exactly this.
#
# Back it up against the running container, never with `cp`: a live database keeps recent writes in a
# write-ahead log beside the file, and copying the .db alone loses them. Measured on a fresh
# container: fruitback.db was 4 KB while fruitback.db-wal held 53 KB.
#   docker compose exec worker sqlite3 /data/fruitback.db ".backup '/data/backup.db'"
#
# SQLite needs a persistent filesystem, so it cannot run on a serverless runtime. That is the trade.
# FRUITBACK_SQLITE_PATH=/data/fruitback.db

# ── FRUITBACK_STORE=linear (the default) ─────────────────────────────────────

# Linear personal API key. The whole reason this service exists: it must never reach the browser.
LINEAR_API_KEY=

# Team SKG in the sakuga-software workspace.
LINEAR_TEAM_ID=d2e5bf16-7c31-4b8b-b45a-bedc845b1517

# Fruitback's own project. A mapped client can override it — see FRUITBACK_CLIENTS.
LINEAR_PROJECT_ID=f984149d-9ecc-4e30-b04c-b8d0d8bea267

# Requests per minute per client IP, reads and writes together. Left unset in production; the E2E
# suite raises it, because every request there comes from the same loopback address.
RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=20

# Reverse proxies in front of the container. 1 = Dokploy's Traefik. Set to 0 only when the container
# is reached directly, and raise it if a CDN is added upstream — too low and the rate-limit key
# becomes caller-controlled.
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

PORT=8080

# Interface to bind. 0.0.0.0 inside a container, which is the default and almost always right —
# 127.0.0.1 would make the service unreachable from Traefik.
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# One worker, several client sites. JSON: clientId → { teamId?, projectId?, origins? }. Absent means
# a single client, which is how this worker behaves by default. Once set, a client has to be named
# on BOTH paths — the `client` parameter on a read, `seed.client.id` on a write — and an unknown one
# is refused. On a shared worker, falling back to the default team is how one client ends up reading
# another's feedback, and how its own notes end up in someone else's team.
#
# `origins` binds a client to the sites it may be embedded on. `clientId` is client-asserted — an
# identity token says who the *reporter* is, not which client the page is — so this stays the
# cheapest check available: the same trust level CORS already gives.
#
# Per client, also: `identitySecret` (its own signing key, see below) and `showComments` (false to
# keep this client's Linear replies off its pages).
# FRUITBACK_CLIENTS={"acme":{"teamId":"team_...","projectId":"proj_...","origins":["https://acme.test"]}}

# Shared with a client site so it can mint identity tokens: a short-lived HS256 JWT in the
# `Authorization` header turns a self-declared name into a verified one. Absent — the default — means
# every reporter is self-declared, which is a perfectly good way to run this. 32 characters minimum,
# because a short HMAC secret is a guessable one.
#
# This is the SINGLE-CLIENT key. A worker with FRUITBACK_CLIENTS ignores it: each client declares its
# own `identitySecret` or has none, because one signing key shared across tenants lets a compromised
# tenant mint verified identities on anyone else's issues.
# FRUITBACK_IDENTITY_SECRET=

# ── The browser extension's sessions (optional) ──────────────────────────────
# Where the extension's sessions are kept. Absent — the default — turns the /session endpoints off,
# which is every worker that has no extension pointed at it.
#
# This is NOT the seed store. Seeds go wherever the team already tracks issues, and neither Linear
# nor GitHub is a place to keep credentials, so a worker on any store still keeps its sessions on a
# disk it owns. Its own file, its own schema — give it the same persistent volume as the seed store:
#
#   volumes:
#     - fruitback-data:/data
#
# Back it up the same way, and for the same reason a plain `cp` is not enough:
#
#   docker compose exec worker sqlite3 /data/sessions.db ".backup '/data/sessions-backup.db'"
#
# It needs FRUITBACK_IDENTITY_SECRET, which is what signs a session's access token — setting this
# without it is refused at boot. It is also refused alongside FRUITBACK_CLIENTS: a session signs with
# the worker-wide key, which a mapped worker ignores, so pairing would work and every read would 401.
#
# Mint a code for somebody with:
#
#   docker compose exec worker node server.mjs pair --subject alice --name "Alice Martin"
#
# FRUITBACK_SESSION_PATH=/data/sessions.db

# Who may read pins: `public` (the default) or `authenticated`.
#
# `public` is what this worker has always done: GET /feedback answers anyone who can build the URL,
# so the notes, their authors and the team's replies are readable by every visitor of the client's
# site — and by curl. Right for anonymous feedback on a public site, wrong for internal review.
#
# `authenticated` requires the same HS256 identity token the write path takes, and answers 401
# without one. It needs FRUITBACK_IDENTITY_SECRET (or a per-client `identitySecret`); asking for it
# with no key is refused at boot rather than left as a permanent 401. A mapped client's own `read`
# overrides this.
#
# The default stays `public` so an upgrade never blanks a working deployment. The boot log names
# every client whose pins anyone can read, and /health counts them.
# FRUITBACK_READ=authenticated

# `1` keeps the team's Linear replies out of the read path. They are shown by default — closing that
# loop is the point. Under FRUITBACK_READ=authenticated this is an editorial choice; under `public`
# it is the only thing between an issue thread and anyone who can load the client's page. A mapped
# client's own `showComments` overrides it.
# FRUITBACK_HIDE_COMMENTS=1

# The older spelling of FRUITBACK_STORE=memory, still honoured. It *degrades* to the real store
# under NODE_ENV=production and says so in the log, where an explicit FRUITBACK_STORE=memory is
# refused at boot instead — a flag a container inherited must not stop it serving, a provider
# somebody named must not be silently swapped. `pnpm dev` uses FRUITBACK_STORE=memory.
# FRUITBACK_FAKE_LINEAR=1
